This is almost certainly at most semi-deliberate as well, as evidenced
by the fact that on several, but not all, platforms NetHack draws blue
characters in bright blue specifically to enhance their visibility.

(I wrote up some of the details at http://nethackwiki.com/wiki/Colors
when I last looked at the code.  Alas, that page is still unfinished,
and the NetHack color handling code itself is rather hard to read.)


>> At least some of the color confusion does seem to be intentional:
>> consider the otherwise completely inexplicable Elvenking on the Plane
>> of Earth.
>
> Elven kings come with a pick axe.

Only the one on the Plane of Earth does.  Normal Elvenkings don't have
axes, and whoever designed the level could just as easily have given
the axe to a monster more likely to have one normally (like a dwarf of
some kind) if they hadn't specifically wanted a purple @.

...oh, wait, I was wrong -- the source says that normal Elvenkings do
have a 2 in 3 chance of having a pick-axe (and, interestingly, a 1 in
50 chance of having a crystal ball).  Tellingly enough, though, that
piece of code was added in NetHack 3.1.0, the same version in which
the elemental planes were introduced.

(Actually, in 3.1.0 all Elvenkings always got a pick-axe in addition
to their normal elven gear, and never got a crystal ball.  I didn't
check to see when that was changed to the current 2-in-3 chance
outside the Plane of Earth.)

